EQUALITY & INCLUSION

Obstacle Sport for Everyone

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) wants obstacle sport to be welcoming, respectful and accessible to as many people as possible.

People come to OCR with different backgrounds, experiences, abilities and sporting ambitions.

Some want to compete internationally.

Others want to complete their first obstacle race, join a club, volunteer, coach or simply train with their local community.

All of these people have a place within the sport.

Our Commitment

OCRA is committed to promoting an environment where people are treated with dignity and respect.

We aim to reduce unnecessary barriers to participation and support opportunities across:

- Recreational participation
- Competitive sport
- Clubs
- Coaching
- Technical Officiating
- Volunteering
- Leadership
- Education
- Team Ireland
- Community activities

Different Backgrounds

Participation in obstacle sport should not be unnecessarily restricted because of a person's:

- Background
- Ethnicity
- Nationality
- Gender
- Disability
- Sexual orientation
- Religion or belief
- Socio-economic circumstances
- Previous sporting experience
- Level of athletic ability

Adaptive and Disability Inclusion

Obstacle sport can be adapted to support athletes with different physical, sensory and other requirements.

OCRA wants adaptive athletes to have meaningful opportunities to:

- Participate
- Train
- Compete
- Develop
- Represent Ireland where appropriate competition pathways exist

Coaches, clubs and organisers should consider accessibility when planning activities and competitions.

Competitive Categories

Inclusive participation does not mean that every participant will necessarily be eligible for every competitive category.

Competition may include eligibility rules relating to:

- Age
- Discipline
- Competitive category
- Classification
- International competition requirements

These rules should be communicated clearly and applied consistently.

Clubs and Coaches

Clubs and coaches play an important role in creating welcoming sporting environments.

They should consider:

- Accessibility
- Appropriate coaching adaptations
- Respectful language
- Athlete welfare
- Different levels of ability
- Barriers to participation
- Inclusive club culture

Discrimination and Harassment

Discriminatory, abusive or harassing behaviour is inconsistent with OCRA's values.

Serious incidents may be considered under the OCRA Complaints and Disciplinary process.

Growing Participation

OCRA wants to work with clubs, race organisers, coaches and community organisations to make obstacle sport accessible to people who may not traditionally see themselves represented within OCR.

Developing a genuinely inclusive sport requires more than simply stating that everybody is welcome.

It requires listening to participants, identifying barriers and continually improving how the sport is delivered.

